{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/15/des.md && sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据每个封闭区域内部\"探针\"像素的颜色，在该区域的边界上找到同色锚点，然后从锚点向区域内部生长出与探针数量等长的射线。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格中有若干由同一种边框色（如 blue）绘制的封闭矩形区域，每个矩形内部为背景色。\n  - 每个矩形的边框上恰好有一个非边框色、非背景色的像素，称为\"锚点\"；锚点的颜色各不相同。\n  - 每个矩形的内部散布着若干孤立的单像素\"探针\"，每个探针的颜色恰好与某个矩形边框上的锚点颜色一致。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（配对）：对于每个矩形，收集其内部所有探针像素。将每个探针按颜色匹配到拥有同色锚点的那个矩形（可以是自身所在的矩形，也可以是其他矩形）。\n  - 第二步（方向推断）：锚点位于矩形边框的哪一条边决定了射线方向——锚点在顶边则射线向下，在底边则向上，在左边则向右，在右边则向左。\n  - 第三步（绘制射线）：在锚点所在矩形内部，从锚点相邻的内部像素开始，沿推断方向绘制一条射线，射线长度等于所有与该锚点颜色匹配的探针总数（跨所有矩形计数）。射线颜色与锚点颜色相同。\n  - 其余像素保持不变，探针像素保留在原位。\n\n- `Key insight`：探针不是要被移动或删除的标记——它们是分布在不同矩形中的\"计数票\"，需要按颜色跨区域汇总后，决定对应锚点射线的长度；而射线的方向则由锚点在边框上的位置隐式编码。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将网格中每个由彩色像素组成的小图案视为\"印章\"，根据印章旁指示箭头的朝向，将印章沿该方向翻转后\"盖印\"到网格对侧的目标区域中。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格被一条贯穿全网格的单像素分隔线（grey）分为左右（或上下）两半。\n  - 一侧包含若干小型多色图案（\"印章\"），每个印章旁边紧邻一个由 red 像素组成的三像素 L 形箭头，箭头开口朝向四个正方向之一（上、下、左、右）。L 形箭头的绘制方式：两个像素组成一条短线段，第三个像素从短线段末端垂直拐弯，整体指示一个方向。\n  - 另一侧为纯背景区域，是印章要被\"盖印\"到的目标区域。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（识别箭头方向）：每个印章的箭头 L 形开口朝向决定翻转轴——箭头朝左或朝右表示水平翻转（左右镜像），箭头朝上或朝下表示垂直翻转（上下镜像）。\n  - 第二步（计算目标位置）：印章翻转后，被放置到分隔线另一侧的镜像对称位置。具体地，将印章的边界框相对于分隔线做镜像反射，得到目标边界框的位置。\n  - 第三步（绘制）：将翻转后的印章绘制到目标位置。箭头本身不被复制到输出中（即箭头在输出中被擦除为背景色）。分隔线保持不变。\n  - 若多个印章的目标区域重叠，后绘制的印章覆盖先绘制的（但在典型题目中不会出现重叠）。\n\n- `Key insight`：箭头的方向不是指示印章的移动方向，而是指示翻转轴的类型——水平方向的箭头触发水平翻转，垂直方向的箭头触发垂直翻转；同时印章的最终位置由其相对于分隔线的镜像决定，而非由箭头方向平移。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在一个包含多个颜色不同的矩形框架的网格中，根据框架内部\"种子\"像素的排列模式，选择正确的填充纹理，然后用框架自身的颜色绘制该纹理填满框架内部。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格中有若干个由不同颜色绘制的单像素厚矩形空心框架（例如 red 框、blue 框、green 框）。每个框架内部区域为背景色，但其中散布着少量 yellow 像素作为\"种子\"。\n  - 网格的一个角落区域（如左上角）有一个\"纹理字典\"：由分隔线划分的若干小格子，每个小格子内用 yellow 像素画出一种纹理样本（如水平条纹、竖直条纹、棋盘格、对角线等），同时每个格子内也有少量 yellow 种子像素的排列模式作为\"签名\"。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（提取签名）：对于纹理字典中的每个格子，提取其中种子像素的相对位置模式（即种子像素在格子内的相对坐标集合），这构成该纹理的\"签名\"。\n  - 第二步（匹配）：对于每个矩形框架，提取其内部种子像素的相对位置模式，与字典中的签名进行匹配（匹配时允许平移对齐，但不允许旋转或翻转）。找到匹配的纹理。\n  - 第三步（填充）：将匹配到的纹理图案以平铺方式填满框架内部，但纹理的颜色不用 yellow，而用该框架本身的边框颜色。种子像素被纹理覆盖。\n  - 纹理字典区域在输出中保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：种子像素的空间排列本身就是一种\"密码\"，它不编码颜色或大小信息，而是通过位置模式索引到字典中的纹理类型；最终填充色则由框架颜色决定，形成\"种子选纹理、框架选颜色\"的双重间接映射。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：网格中散布着若干对颜色相同的小对象，每对之间用同色的路径像素相连；根据路径的转弯次数决定该对对象在输出中是否保留，并用转弯次数决定对象的重绘大小。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格中有若干种非背景颜色，每种颜色恰好出现两个小型矩形对象（如 2x2 方块），以及一条连接这两个对象的单像素宽路径（同色）。路径沿水平或垂直方向延伸，可以有若干次 90 度转弯。\n  - 不同颜色的路径不会交叉或接触。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（识别路径与转弯）：对于每种颜色，找到连接两个同色对象的路径，统计路径中 90 度转弯的次数（即方向改变的次数）。\n  - 第二步（过滤）：仅保留转弯次数为奇数的颜色对；转弯次数为偶数（包括 0 次，即直线连接）的颜色对整体擦除为背景色。\n  - 第三步（重绘对象）：对于保留的每对对象，将两个对象都重绘为边长等于（转弯次数 + 1）的实心正方形，正方形以原对象的中心为中心绘制，颜色不变。路径像素在输出中被擦除为背景色。\n  - 若重绘后的正方形超出网格边界，则截断到网格范围内。\n\n- `Key insight`：路径不是装饰性连线——它的几何复杂度（转弯次数）同时充当\"筛选条件\"（奇偶性决定保留或删除）和\"尺寸参数\"（决定保留对象的放大倍数），形成一条信息的双重用途。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在一个被分隔线划分成多个房间的网格中，每个房间里有一个彩色\"钥匙\"像素和若干灰色\"门\"像素；钥匙的颜色决定哪些门被打开（变为背景），打开门后相邻房间合并，合并后的大房间用其中面积最大的原始房间的钥匙颜色重新涂满所有墙壁。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格被 maroon 色的单像素厚墙壁分隔为若干矩形房间。\n  - 每个房间内部恰好有一个非背景、非墙壁色的单像素，称为\"钥匙\"，颜色各异（如 red、blue、green、yellow 等）。\n  - 每个房间的墙壁上有若干像素被替换为 grey（\"门\"像素）。每扇门旁边（在门的房间外侧、相邻房间内侧）有一个与某个钥匙颜色相同的单像素标记，表示该门需要该颜色的钥匙才能打开。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（开门）：对于每个房间，检查其钥匙颜色。找到所有标记颜色与该钥匙匹配的门（门旁标记色 = 钥匙色），将这些门的 grey 像素替换为背景色（即打开门）。同时擦除门旁的颜色标记。\n  - 第二步（合并房间）：打开门后，通过被打开的门洞相连的房间合并为一个大房间。\n  - 第三步（重涂墙壁）：对于每个合并后的大房间（或未合并的单独房间），找到其中面积最大的原始子房间（面积 = 内部背景像素数），将该大房间所有剩余墙壁（maroon 像素）重涂为该最大子房间的钥匙颜色。钥匙像素本身在输出中被擦除为背景色。\n  - 未被打开的门（grey 像素）保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题需要三层非平凡推理——首先理解钥匙-门的颜色匹配机制决定哪些墙壁被打通，然后理解打通后的连通性决定房间合并关系，最后理解合并后的墙壁重涂规则取决于各子房间面积的比较。每一层都需要从样例中独立归纳。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：网格中有若干个单色对象，每个对象的轮廓上恰好有一个缺口；将每个对象沿缺口方向\"推出\"一个副本，副本的颜色由缺口所朝向的边界上最近的调色板色块决定。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格的四条边界上分布着若干单像素色块（非背景色），作为\"调色板\"。每个色块颜色各不相同。\n  - 网格内部有若干个单色（如 grey）实心矩形对象，每个对象的边界上恰好有一个像素被擦除为背景色，形成一个\"缺口\"。缺口一定位于对象的某条边上（上、下、左或右边）。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（判断缺口方向）：对于每个对象，找到其边界上的缺口位置。缺口所在的边决定了方向——缺口在对象顶边则方向为上，底边则方向为下，左边则方向为左，右边则方向为右。\n  - 第二步（查找调色板）：从缺口位置出发，沿缺口方向直线延伸到网格边界，找到该方向边界上最近的调色板色块（即与缺口在同一行或同一列上的边界色块）。该色块的颜色即为副本颜色。\n  - 第三步（生成副本）：将原始对象（含缺口）沿缺口方向平移，平移距离等于对象在该方向上的尺寸（即对象的高度或宽度），在新位置绘制一个相同形状（含缺口）的副本，副本颜色为第二步找到的调色板颜色。\n  - 原始对象保持不变（包括缺口）。若副本位置超出网格，则截断。\n\n- `Key insight`：缺口不是需要被修补的瑕疵，而是一个方向指示器——它同时编码了副本的平移方向和调色板的查找方向；对象本身的颜色与副本颜色无关，副本颜色完全由缺口指向的边界色块决定。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：网格中有一个由多种颜色像素组成的大型图案和若干个小型单色\"滤镜框\"；将大图案中落在每个滤镜框投影区域内的像素按特定规则筛选后，填入滤镜框内部。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格中央有一个大型多色图案（由多种非背景颜色组成的任意形状）。\n  - 网格边缘附近有若干个小型单像素厚的矩形空心框（\"滤镜框\"），每个框的颜色不同。每个滤镜框的内部为纯背景色。\n  - 每个滤镜框的宽度和高度恰好与大图案的宽度和高度相同。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（投影对齐）：将每个滤镜框的内部区域与大图案的边界框一一对齐（即滤镜框内部的第 i 行第 j 列对应大图案边界框的第 i 行第 j 列）。\n  - 第二步（颜色筛选）：对于每个滤镜框，仅保留大图案中与该滤镜框边框颜色相同的像素。即：在对齐后的映射中，如果大图案某位置的颜色等于该滤镜框的边框颜色，则在滤镜框内部对应位置绘制该颜色；否则保持背景色。\n  - 大图案本身在输出中保持不变。滤镜框的边框在输出中保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：滤镜框看似是需要被\"填入内容\"的容器，但它的边框颜色实际上是一个选择指令——它从共享的大图案中提取出属于自己颜色的那一层，相当于对多色图案做\"按颜色分层\"操作。解题者需要意识到框的颜色不是装饰，而是筛选条件。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在一个包含若干彩色条带和散落标记点的网格中，每个标记点\"激活\"与其同行或同列的条带片段，被激活的片段旋转90度后重新放置在标记点的位置。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为主色（black）。\n  - 网格中有若干条水平或垂直的单像素厚彩色条带（每条条带为单一颜色，不同条带颜色可以不同）。条带贯穿网格的大部分宽度或高度，但不一定到达边界。\n  - 网格中散布着若干 yellow 单像素标记点，每个标记点恰好与一条条带在同一行（对于水平条带）或同一列（对于垂直条带）上，但标记点不在条带上。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步（关联）：每个 yellow 标记点关联到与其共享行或列的那条条带。\n  - 第二步（截取片段）：从关联条带中截取一个片段——片段以标记点在条带方向上的投影位置为中心，长度等于标记点到条带最近端点的距离（以像素计）。\n  - 第三步（旋转放置）：将截取的片段旋转 90 度（水平片段变垂直，垂直片段变水平），以标记点为中心放置到网格上。旋转后的片段颜色与原条带相同。\n  - 第四步（清理）：原条带中被截取的片段部分擦除为背景色。标记点本身在输出中也被擦除为背景色（被旋转后的片段像素覆盖或擦除）。未被截取的条带部分保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：标记点不仅指示\"哪条条带被操作\"，还通过自身与条带端点的距离隐式编码了截取长度——这意味着同一条条带上不同位置的标记点会截取不同长度的片段。解题者需要发现截取长度的计算规则依赖于标记点的相对位置而非某个固定常数。\n</new_puzzle>\n你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9473c6fb\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRecolor the sparse foreground pixels in scan order along their longer spread, cycling red, teal, and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Every foreground object is a single isolated 4-connected pixel.\n- The foreground pixel set has a rectangular bbox whose row or column extent defines the scan direction.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- If the foreground bbox is at least as tall as it is wide, order foreground pixels row-major; otherwise order them column-major.\n- Recolor the ordered pixels by repeating red, teal, grey from the first pixel onward.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground colors are only markers; the output color depends on each marker's ordinal position in the dominant scan direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9ba4a9aa\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReturn the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.\n\nInput structure\n- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,\n  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.\n\nRule\n- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.\n- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.\n- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.\n\nKey insight\n- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide\n  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5587a8d0\" lines=\"11-27\">\n<description>\nRedraw foreground objects as nested square rings ordered by object size.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected monochromatic component.\n- Foreground object areas are distinct.\n\nRule:\n- Sort the foreground objects by decreasing pixel count.\n- Create a square output with side length 2 * number_of_objects - 1.\n- The largest object color fills the outer ring, each next object color fills\n  the next inner ring, and the smallest object color fills the center pixel.\n\nKey insight: The objects' original shapes do not get copied; their areas\ndetermine only the outside-to-inside order of the nested square pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ff2825db\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nSelect the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.\n- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.\n- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has\n  the unique highest count.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the palette row unchanged.\n- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.\n- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,\n  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.\n\nKey insight:\n- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker\n  color and its spatial extent define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f5f939b\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nMark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.\n- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.\n- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.\n- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\nRule:\n- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.\n- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.\n- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.\n\nKey insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db7260a4\" lines=\"37-56\">\n<description>\nDrop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,\n  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.\n- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous\n  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top marker from its original cell.\n- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its\n  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.\n- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row\n  with the marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop\n  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8cc533f\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nComplete every colored sprite by applying the union of all aligned partial sprite masks.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each non-background color appears as a separated partial copy of one common sprite mask.\n- The copies share a top-left-aligned sprite window; some copies may be missing lower or right edge pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop each same-color mask to its bounding box.\n- Pad the crops to the maximum observed height and width, aligned at the top-left, and OR them into one complete template.\n- Fill that template at every sprite location using the sprite's own color.\n\nKey insight:\n- No single input sprite is complete, but their missing cells are complementary, so the aligned union reveals the full mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6a980be1\" lines=\"46-61\">\n<description>\nProject the framed periodic stripe pattern across the canvas and erase the unused frame cells.\n\nInput structure\n- A bg canvas with a one-cell-thick rectangular frame of one color.\n- Inside the frame are two parallel full-interior marker bars of another color.\n- A third color fills the region between the marker bars on periodically spaced rows or columns.\n\nRule\n- Infer the periodic row or column mask from the third-color stripes inside the frame.\n- Paint the frame color along those full rows or columns, extended to the grid boundary.\n- Extend the marker bars across the full canvas and repaint the source-color stripe cells between them.\n\nKey insight\n- The outer frame is not preserved as a border; it supplies the color for the periodic stripes selected by the inner source pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\" lines=\"120-133\">\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"dce56571\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nStraighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.\n- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Erase the original foreground mask.\n- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.\n- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b10624e5.py && sed -n '369,460p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b10624e5.py && sed -n '1,130p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py && sed -n '604,700p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b10624e5.py && sed -n '369,460p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b10624e5.py && sed -n '1,130p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py && sed -n '604,700p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py\"","aggregated_output":"import numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import bbox_coords, components_mask, get_bg, paste\nimport random\n\n\ndef _find_dividers(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the unique full-row/full-col quadrant divider: (mid_row, mid_col).\n    \"\"\"\n    h, w = g.shape\n    full_rows = [(r, int(g[r, 0])) for r in range(h) if np.all(g[r, :] == g[r, 0]) and int(g[r, 0]) != bg]\n    full_cols = [(c, int(g[0, c])) for c in range(w) if np.all(g[:, c] == g[0, c]) and int(g[0, c]) != bg]\n    matches = []\n    for r, vr in full_rows:\n        for c, vc in full_cols:\n            if vr == vc:\n                matches.append((int(r), int(c)))\n    assert len(matches) == 1, \"grid must have exactly one matching full-row/full-col divider\"\n    return matches[0]\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Replicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\n    - Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\n    - The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Use the top-left quadrant's motif as the source pattern.\n    - For each other quadrant, scale the motif by the ratio of that quadrant's red block size to the source red block size.\n    - Reflect horizontally for right-side quadrants, vertically for bottom quadrants (both for bottom-right).\n    - Align the scaled/reflected motif so its red region overlaps the target anchor, then stamp non-bg pixels.\n\n    Key insight: Red block size encodes the scale factor; quadrant position encodes the reflection axes.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    h, w = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    mid_r, mid_c = _find_dividers(g, bg)\n\n    # Find red anchor blocks (color 2) in each quadrant.\n    red = components_mask(g == 2, connectivity=4)\n    assert red, \"grid must contain red (color 2) anchor blocks\"\n\n    # Quadrant key: (top/bottom, left/right)\n    anchors: dict[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int, int]] = {}\n    # value: (r0,c0,size)\n    for pts in red:\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n        rr = (r0 + r1) // 2\n        cc = (c0 + c1) // 2\n        qb = 0 if rr < mid_r else 1\n        qr = 0 if cc < mid_c else 1\n        size = max(r1 - r0 + 1, c1 - c0 + 1)\n        key = (qb, qr)\n        assert key not in anchors, \"each quadrant must contain exactly one red anchor block\"\n        anchors[key] = (int(r0), int(c0), int(size))\n\n    assert (0, 0) in anchors, \"top-left quadrant must contain a red anchor block\"\n    sr0, sc0, sL = anchors[(0, 0)]\n    assert sL > 0, \"top-left red anchor must have positive size\"\n\n    # Build a source patch around the TL motif: take the non-bg connected component (4-neigh)\n    # in the TL quadrant that contains the TL red anchor.\n    tl = g[0:mid_r, 0:mid_c]\n    comps = components_mask(tl != bg, connectivity=4)\n    patch_bb = None\n    for pts in comps:\n        # component contains any red pixel?\n        if any(tl[r, c] == 2 for r, c in pts):\n            r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n            patch_bb = (int(r0), int(c0), int(r1), int(c1))\n            break\n    assert patch_bb is not None, \"top-left quadrant must contain a non-bg component with a red pixel\"\n\n    pr0, pc0, pr1, pc1 = patch_bb\n    patch = tl[pr0 : pr1 + 1, pc0 : pc1 + 1].copy()\n\n    def resize_nn(img: np.ndarray, new_h: int, new_w: int) -> np.ndarray:\n        src_h, src_w = img.shape\n        if new_h <= 0 or new_w <= 0:\n            return img.copy()\n        rr = (np.floor(np.arange(new_h) * src_h / new_h)).astype(int)\n        cc = (np.floor(np.arange(new_w) * src_w / new_w)).astype(int)\n        rr = np.clip(rr, 0, src_h - 1)\n        cc = np.clip(cc, 0, src_w - 1)\n        return img[rr[:, None], cc[None, :]]\n\n    # For each quadrant anchor, paste a scaled/reflected version of the patch.\n    for (qb, qr), (tr0, tc0, tL) in anchors.items():\n        if (qb, qr) == (0, 0):\n            continue\n        patch_t = patch\n        if qr == 1:\n            patch_t = np.fliplr(patch_t)\n        if qb == 1:\n            patch_t = np.flipud(patch_t)\n\n        ratio = float(tL) / float(sL)\n        new_h = int(round(patch_t.shape[0] * ratio))\n        new_w = int(round(patch_t.shape[1] * ratio))\n        new_h = max(1, new_h)\n        new_w = max(1, new_w)\n        patch_s = resize_nn(patch_t, new_h, new_w)\n        roundtrip = resize_nn(patch_s, patch_t.shape[0], patch_t.shape[1])\n        assert np.array_equal(roundtrip, patch_t), \"scaling must preserve all motif information\"\n\n        # Align scaled red bbox in patch_s to the target red bbox.\n        rr_red, cc_red = np.where(patch_s == 2)\n        assert rr_red.size > 0, \"scaled source patch must contain red anchor pixels\"\n        ar0, ac0 = int(rr_red.min()), int(cc_red.min())\n        place_r0 = tr0 - ar0\n        place_c0 = tc0 - ac0\n\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(MAX_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid b10624e5 input\")\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import bbox_coords, components_mask, get_bg, neighbors4\n\n\ndef _legend_directions(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> dict[int, tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Detect legend blocks on the border.\n\n    A legend is a border-touching non-background component whose most common color is the legend\n    background `C` (and also the region border color it controls). The legend contains at least\n    two additional colors: a \"center\" color (more frequent) and an \"indicator\" color (less frequent).\n\n    The movement direction for regions with border color `C` is:\n      sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center))\n    \"\"\"\n    h, w = g.shape\n    non_bg = g != bg\n    best: dict[int, tuple[int, tuple[int, int]]] = {}\n    for pts in components_mask(non_bg, connectivity=4):\n        if not any(r == 0 or c == 0 or r == h - 1 or c == w - 1 for r, c in pts):\n            continue\n        # Legends are filled rectangles on the border.\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n        area = (r1 - r0 + 1) * (c1 - c0 + 1)\n        if area != len(pts):\n            continue\n\n        counts: dict[int, int] = {}\n        for r, c in pts:\n            v = int(g[r, c])\n            counts[v] = counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n        if len(counts) != 3:\n            continue\n\n        bg_col = max(counts, key=counts.get)\n        others = [(cnt, col) for col, cnt in counts.items() if col != bg_col]\n        if len(others) < 2:\n            continue\n        others.sort()  # ascending by count\n        if others[0][0] == others[1][0]:\n            # Tie: choose center as the color closer to the rectangle center.\n            rc = np.array([(r0 + r1) / 2.0, (c0 + c1) / 2.0])\n            cols = [others[0][1], others[1][1]]\n            cents = []\n            for col in cols:\n                pts_col = np.array([(r, c) for r, c in pts if int(g[r, c]) == col], dtype=float)\n                cents.append((np.sum((pts_col.mean(axis=0) - rc) ** 2), col))\n            cents.sort()\n            center_col = cents[0][1]\n            indicator_col = cents[1][1]\n        else:\n            indicator_col = others[0][1]\n            center_col = others[-1][1]\n\n        center = [(r, c) for r, c in pts if int(g[r, c]) == center_col]\n        indic = [(r, c) for r, c in pts if int(g[r, c]) == indicator_col]\n        if not center or not indic:\n            continue\n\n        cc = np.mean(np.array(center, dtype=float), axis=0)\n        ci = np.mean(np.array(indic, dtype=float), axis=0)\n        vr, vc = (ci - cc).tolist()\n        dr = 0 if abs(vr) < 1e-6 else (1 if vr > 0 else -1)\n        dc = 0 if abs(vc) < 1e-6 else (1 if vc > 0 else -1)\n        if dr == 0 and dc == 0:\n            continue\n\n        prev = best.get(bg_col)\n        if prev is None or len(pts) > prev[0]:\n            best[bg_col] = (len(pts), (dr, dc))\n\n    return {k: v for k, (_, v) in best.items()}\n\n\ndef _slide_points(region_set: set[tuple[int, int]], points: list[tuple[int, int]], dr: int, dc: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    if not points:\n        return []\n    # Process from the far end (closest to destination boundary) inward.\n    points_sorted = sorted(points, key=lambda p: (dr * p[0] + dc * p[1], p[0], p[1]), reverse=True)\n    occupied = set()\n    out_pts = []\n    for r0, c0 in points_sorted:\n        r, c = r0, c0\n        while True:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if (nr, nc) not in region_set:\n                break\n            # For diagonal steps, disallow \"corner cutting\": both orthogonal intermediates must be inside the region.\n            if dr != 0 and dc != 0:\n                if (r + dr, c) not in region_set or (r, c + dc) not in region_set:\n                    break\n            if (nr, nc) in occupied:\n                break\n            r, c = nr, nc\n        occupied.add((r, c))\n        out_pts.append((r, c))\n    return out_pts\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n    - Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n    - The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n    - All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\n    Key insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    h, w = g.shape\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n\n    mover = 4  # yellow in ARC\n    dirs = _legend_directions(g, bg)\n    assert dirs, \"legend_directions must find at least one legend; grid should always have border legends\"\n\n    passable = (g == bg) | (g == mover)\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    # Find enclosed passable regions (not connected to the outer border).\n    for pts in components_mask(passable, connectivity=4):\n        if any(r == 0 or c == 0 or r == h - 1 or c == w - 1 for r, c in pts):\n            continue  # outside background\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    rng = random.Random()\n    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n        try:\n            g = _gen_attempt(rng)\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        if g is not None:\n            return g\n    raise RuntimeError(\"generate_input: exceeded retries\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py && sed -n '251,340p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py && sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3bd292e8.py && sed -n '452,560p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3bd292e8.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py && sed -n '251,340p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py && sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3bd292e8.py && sed -n '452,560p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3bd292e8.py\"","aggregated_output":"import numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import bbox_h, bbox_w, crop_bbox, find_components, get_bg, paste\nimport random\n\n\ndef _center(obj: dict, axis: int) -> float:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = obj[\"bbox\"]\n    return ((r0 + r1) / 2, (c0 + c1) / 2)[axis]\n\n\ndef _span(obj: dict, axis: int) -> int:\n    return bbox_h(obj[\"bbox\"]) if axis == 0 else bbox_w(obj[\"bbox\"])\n\n\ndef _is_solid_rectangle(g: np.ndarray, obj: dict) -> bool:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = obj[\"bbox\"]\n    color = int(obj[\"color\"])\n    return (\n        int(obj[\"area\"]) == (r1 - r0 + 1) * (c1 - c0 + 1)\n        and np.all(g[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1] == color)\n    )\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Push the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\n    Input structure\n    - The background is the dominant color.\n    - There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n      horizontal or vertical axis.\n    - The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n    - One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n      to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\n    Rule\n    - Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n      flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n    - Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n    - Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n      shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n    - Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\n    Key insight\n    - The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n      away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n\n    objects = find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True)\n    assert len(objects) == 3, \"expected exactly three foreground objects\"\n\n    row_centers = [_center(obj, 0) for obj in objects]\n    col_centers = [_center(obj, 1) for obj in objects]\n    row_spread = max(row_centers) - min(row_centers)\n    col_spread = max(col_centers) - min(col_centers)\n    assert min(row_spread, col_spread) <= 0.5, \"objects must share a row or column axis\"\n    axis = 0 if col_spread <= 0.5 else 1\n    perp = 1 - axis\n\n    ordered = sorted(objects, key=lambda obj: _center(obj, axis))\n    axis_centers = [_center(obj, axis) for obj in ordered]\n    assert axis_centers[0] < axis_centers[1] < axis_centers[2], \"expected a unique middle object\"\n\n    source = ordered[1]\n    end_objects = [ordered[0], ordered[2]]\n    assert _is_solid_rectangle(g, source), \"moving object must be a solid rectangle\"\n\n    target_candidates = [\n        obj\n        for obj in end_objects\n        if _is_solid_rectangle(g, obj) and _span(obj, perp) > _span(obj, axis)\n    ]\n    assert len(target_candidates) == 1, \"expected one perpendicular solid target\"\n    target = target_candidates[0]\n\n    direction = -1 if _center(target, axis) < _center(source, axis) else 1\n\n    sr0, sc0, sr1, sc1 = source[\"bbox\"]\n    tr0, tc0, tr1, tc1 = target[\"bbox\"]\n    source_lo = [sr0, sc0]\n    source_hi = [sr1, sc1]\n    target_lo = [tr0, tc0]\n    target_hi = [tr1, tc1]\n\n    source_perp_len = source_hi[perp] - source_lo[perp] + 1\n    assert source_perp_len == 2, \"moving object path must be two cells wide\"\n    assert target_lo[perp] <= source_lo[perp] <= source_hi[perp] <= target_hi[perp]\n    assert (\n        source_lo[perp] - target_lo[perp] == target_hi[perp] - source_hi[perp]\n    ), \"moving object must be centered across the target\"\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for r, c in source[\"coords\"]:\n        out[int(r), int(c)] = bg\n    for r, c in target[\"coords\"]:\n        out[int(r), int(c)] = bg\n\n    shift = source_perp_len // 2\n    for r, c in target[\"coords\"]:\n        new_rc = [int(r), int(c)]\n        if new_rc[perp] <= source_lo[perp]:\n            new_rc[perp] -= shift\n        else:\n            assert new_rc[perp] >= source_hi[perp]\n            new_rc[perp] += shift\n        assert 0 <= new_rc[0] < H and 0 <= new_rc[1] < W, \"shifted target must stay in bounds\"\n        out[new_rc[0], new_rc[1]] = int(target[\"color\"])\n\n    source_patch = crop_bbox(g, source[\"bbox\"])\n    if axis == 0:\n        new_r = 0 if direction < 0 else H - source_patch.shape[0]\n        new_c = source_lo[1]\n    else:\n        new_r = source_lo[0]\n        new_c = 0 if direction < 0 else W - source_patch.shape[1]\n    dest = out[new_r : new_r + source_patch.shape[0], new_c : new_c + source_patch.shape[1]]\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GEN_RETRY_LIMIT):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid 9f669b64 input\")\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import Color, get_bg, label_mask, neighbors4\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\n    Input structure\n    - The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.\n    - The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.\n    - Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.\n    Rule\n    - Keep all wall pixels unchanged.\n    - Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they\n      touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.\n    - Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between\n      grey and green across every wall adjacency.\n    Key insight\n    - The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background\n      region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2\n    H, W = g.shape\n\n    colors = set(map(int, g.flat))\n    assert len(colors) == 2, \"expected exactly one background color and one wall color\"\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    wall_colors = colors - {int(bg)}\n    assert len(wall_colors) == 1\n    wall = int(next(iter(wall_colors)))\n\n    bg_lab, n_regions = label_mask(g == int(bg), connectivity=4)\n    assert n_regions >= 2, \"walls must split the background into multiple regions\"\n    start_region = int(bg_lab[H - 1, 0])\n    assert start_region > 0, \"lower-left corner must belong to a background region\"\n\n    wall_lab, n_walls = label_mask(g == wall, connectivity=4)\n    assert n_walls >= 1\n\n    graph: dict[int, set[int]] = defaultdict(set)\n    for wall_id in range(1, n_walls + 1):\n        adjacent_regions: set[int] = set()\n        for r, c in np.argwhere(wall_lab == wall_id):\n            for rr, cc in neighbors4(int(r), int(c)):\n                if 0 <= rr < H and 0 <= cc < W:\n                    region_id = int(bg_lab[rr, cc])\n                    if region_id > 0:\n                        adjacent_regions.add(region_id)\n\n        assert len(adjacent_regions) == 2, \"each wall segment must separate exactly two regions\"\n        a, b = tuple(adjacent_regions)\n        graph[a].add(b)\n        graph[b].add(a)\n\n    parity = {start_region: 0}\n    queue = deque([start_region])\n    while queue:\n        region = queue.popleft()\n        for neighbor in graph[region]:\n            neighbor_parity = 1 - parity[region]\n            if neighbor in parity:\n                assert parity[neighbor] == neighbor_parity, \"region graph must be bipartite\"\n            else:\n                parity[neighbor] = neighbor_parity\n                queue.append(neighbor)\n\n    assert set(parity) == set(range(1, n_regions + 1)), \"all background regions must be wall-connected\"\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for region_id, region_parity in parity.items():\n        out[bg_lab == region_id] = Color.GREY if region_parity == 0 else Color.GREEN\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (att2, o5x) ---------------\n\n# Grid, colors, and retry policy.\nGRID_SIZES = (7, 8, 9)\nGRID_SIZES_FOR_THREE_WALLS = (8, 9)\nWALL_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 3)\nMAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS = 300\nBACKGROUND_COLOR = Color.ORANGE\nWALL_COLOR = Color.RED\n\n# Wall geometry constraints.\nWALL_LENGTH_RANGE = (4, 13)\nWALL_TURN_RANGE = (1, 5)\nENDPOINT_SPACING = 2\nMAX_STAIR_PARTS = 3\nMIN_FIRST_STAIR_COL = 1\n\n# Left-right corridor layout.\nLR_LAYOUT_MAX_WALLS = 2\nLR_LAYOUT_PROBABILITY = 0.55\nLR_TURN_OPTIONS = (2, 4)\nLR_CORRIDOR_MAX_SPAN = 3\nLR_CORRIDOR_GAP = 2\n\n# Canonical grids are randomly mapped through one of eight dihedral transforms.\nTRANSFORM_OPS = 8\n\n\ndef _contains(bounds: tuple[int, int], value: int) -> bool:\n    lo, hi = bounds\n    return lo <= int(value) <= hi\n\n\ndef _paint_wall_path(grid: np.ndarray, cells: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> bool:\n    for r, c in cells:\n        if grid[r, c] != BACKGROUND_COLOR:\n            return False\n        grid[r, c] = WALL_COLOR\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate valid 3bd292e8 input\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2c181942.py && sed -n '535,650p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2c181942.py && sed -n '119,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py && sed -n '475,590p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2c181942.py && sed -n '535,650p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2c181942.py && sed -n '119,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py && sed -n '475,590p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\"","aggregated_output":"from __future__ import annotations\nimport random\nimport numpy as np\nfrom arc_utils import STRUCT8, bbox_coords, get_bg, label_mask, paste, random_free_location\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Attach monochrome sprites to the matching sides of a central hollow square.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = background. A hollow NxN square (hub) with bg corners/interior and four differently-colored edges (top, bottom, left, right).\n    - 2-4 monochrome sprites scattered on the grid, each matching one hub edge color.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each hub edge color, find the matching sprite outside the hub.\n    - Rotate the sprite (0/90/180/270°) so its contact edge has exactly N-2 contiguous cells aligned with the hub side, and the rotated sprite is mirror-symmetric along the contact-edge axis (UD for left/right, LR for top/bottom).\n    - Translate it to adjoin that hub side. \n    - Clear everything else to bg; output is hub + attached sprites only.\n\n    Key insight: Each edge color uniquely pairs a sprite to a side; symmetry along the contact-edge axis disambiguates the correct rotation.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n\n    def _find_hub():\n        best = None  # (score, (r,c,n), (top,bot,left,right))\n        for n in range(3, 7):\n            for r in range(0, int(H - n + 1)):\n                for c in range(0, int(W - n + 1)):\n                    # corners must be bg\n                    if (\n                        int(g[int(r), int(c)]) != int(bg)\n                        or int(g[int(r), int(c + n - 1)]) != int(bg)\n                        or int(g[int(r + n - 1), int(c)]) != int(bg)\n                        or int(g[int(r + n - 1), int(c + n - 1)]) != int(bg)\n                    ):\n                        continue\n                    inner = g[int(r + 1) : int(r + n - 1), int(c + 1) : int(c + n - 1)]\n                    if not bool((inner == int(bg)).all()):\n                        continue\n\n                    top = g[int(r), int(c + 1) : int(c + n - 1)]\n                    bot = g[int(r + n - 1), int(c + 1) : int(c + n - 1)]\n                    left = g[int(r + 1) : int(r + n - 1), int(c)]\n                    right = g[int(r + 1) : int(r + n - 1), int(c + n - 1)]\n\n                    if bool((top == int(bg)).any()) or bool((bot == int(bg)).any()) or bool((left == int(bg)).any()) or bool((right == int(bg)).any()):\n                        continue\n                    if (\n                        len(set(map(int, top))) != 1\n                        or len(set(map(int, bot))) != 1\n                        or len(set(map(int, left))) != 1\n                        or len(set(map(int, right))) != 1\n                    ):\n                        continue\n\n                    cr = float(r + (n - 1) / 2)\n                    cc = float(c + (n - 1) / 2)\n                    score = abs(float(cr - (H - 1) / 2)) + abs(float(cc - (W - 1) / 2))\n                    cand = (\n                        float(score),\n                        (int(r), int(c), int(n)),\n                        (int(top[0]), int(bot[0]), int(left[0]), int(right[0])),\n                    )\n                    if best is None or float(cand[0]) < float(best[0]):\n                        best = cand\n        return best\n\n    hub = _find_hub()\n    assert not (hub is None)\n\n    _score, (r, c, n), (top_col, bot_col, left_col, right_col) = hub\n    hub_mask = np.zeros((H, W), dtype=bool)\n    hub_mask[int(r) : int(r + n), int(c) : int(c + n)] = True\n\n    out = np.full_like(g, int(bg))\n    out[int(r) : int(r + n), int(c) : int(c + n)] = g[int(r) : int(r + n), int(c) : int(c + n)]\n\n    target = int(n - 2)  # length of the hub edge segment (excluding corners)\n\n    def _crop_to_mask(arr: np.ndarray, mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n        pts = np.argwhere(mask)\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n        return arr[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1], mask[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1]\n\n    def _edge_segment(mask: np.ndarray, side: str):\n        pts = np.argwhere(mask)\n        if pts.size == 0:\n            return None\n        if side == \"top\":\n            rr = int(pts[:, 0].min())\n            cols = sorted(int(x) for x in pts[pts[:, 0] == rr][:, 1])\n            return (\"h\", int(rr), cols)\n        if side == \"bot\":\n            rr = int(pts[:, 0].max())\n            cols = sorted(int(x) for x in pts[pts[:, 0] == rr][:, 1])\n            return (\"h\", int(rr), cols)\n        if side == \"left\":\n            cc = int(pts[:, 1].min())\n            rows = sorted(int(x) for x in pts[pts[:, 1] == cc][:, 0])\n            return (\"v\", int(cc), rows)\n        if side == \"right\":\n            cc = int(pts[:, 1].max())\n            rows = sorted(int(x) for x in pts[pts[:, 1] == cc][:, 0])\n            return (\"v\", int(cc), rows)\n        return None\n\n    def _is_contig(seq: list[int]) -> bool:\n        return bool(seq) and all(int(seq[i]) + 1 == int(seq[i + 1]) for i in range(len(seq) - 1))\n\n    # desired attachment geometry: adjacent (touching) the hub side segment\n    segs = {\n        \"top\": (int(top_col), (\"bot\", int(r - 1), list(range(int(c + 1), int(c + n - 1))))),\n        \"bot\": (int(bot_col), (\"top\", int(r + n), list(range(int(c + 1), int(c + n - 1))))),\n        \"left\": (int(left_col), (\"right\", int(c - 1), list(range(int(r + 1), int(r + n - 1))))),\n        \"right\": (int(right_col), (\"left\", int(c + n), list(range(int(r + 1), int(r + n - 1))))),\n    }\n\n    for _side, (col, (need_edge, fixed, span)) in segs.items():\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(300):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(\"generate_input: failed after 300 attempts\")\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n    - The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n    - Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n    - Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n    - The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n    - Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n    assert bg == 0\n\n    counts: list[tuple[int, int, np.ndarray]] = []\n    for color in colors_present(g, exclude={bg}):\n        rooms = _room_count_for_color(g, int(color))\n        walls = _walls_for_color(g, int(color))\n        counts.append((int(color), int(rooms), walls))\n\n    drawings = [(color, rooms, walls) for color, rooms, walls in counts if rooms > 0]\n    pad_colors = [color for color, rooms, _walls in counts if rooms == 0]\n    assert drawings\n    assert len(pad_colors) == 1\n\n    pad = int(pad_colors[0])\n    ordered: list[tuple[int, int, np.ndarray]] = []\n    for rooms in sorted({rooms for _color, rooms, _walls in drawings}):\n        group = [item for item in drawings if item[1] == rooms]\n        ordered.extend(_cover_order(g, group))\n\n    width = max(rooms for _color, rooms, _walls in ordered)\n\n    out = np.full((len(ordered), width), pad, dtype=int)\n    for r, (color, rooms, _walls) in enumerate(ordered):\n        out[r, :rooms] = int(color)\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (claude-opus-4-6) ---------------\n\n# ---- CFG: Tunable generation parameters ----\n_FRAME_COUNT_RANGE = (3, 5)           # number of colored frames per puzzle\n_ROOM_COUNT_RANGE = (1, 6)            # rooms per frame\n_TIE_PROBABILITY = 0.35              # chance of one tied room-count pair\n_GRID_SIZE_BY_FRAMES = {             # (h_min, h_max, w_min, w_max, frame_max_h, frame_max_w)\n    5: (25, 30, 25, 30, 16, 18),\n    4: (22, 30, 22, 30, 18, 22),\n    3: (20, 30, 20, 30, 22, 25),\n}\n_CELL_GAP_RANGE = (3, 13)            # min/max gap between grid lines within a frame\n_NOISE_FRACTION_RANGE = (0.04, 0.09) # fraction of total cells as noise\n_WALL_DAMAGE_FRACTION = 0.25         # max fraction of wall pixels replaced by noise\n_EXTRA_OVERLAP_RANGE = (0, 3)        # non-tied overlap pairs to force\n_OVERLAP_RETRY = 100                 # attempts to place overlapping frame\n_GEN_RETRIES = 300                   # outer retry limit\n\n\ndef _random_gaps(n: int, min_gap: int, max_gap: int, max_total: int) -> list[int] | None:\n    \"\"\"Generate n gaps each in [min_gap, max_gap] with sum <= max_total.\"\"\"\n    if n * min_gap > max_total:\n        return None\n    gaps = [min_gap] * n\n    remaining = max_total - sum(gaps)\n    for i in range(n):\n        extra = min(max_gap - gaps[i], remaining)\n        if extra > 0:\n            add = random.randint(0, extra)\n            gaps[i] += add\n            remaining -= add\n    random.shuffle(gaps)\n    return gaps\n\n\ndef _build_frame(target_rooms: int, max_h: int = 22, max_w: int = 25) -> np.ndarray | None:\n    \"\"\"Build a boolean wall array with exactly target_rooms enclosed regions.\"\"\"\n    if target_rooms == 1:\n        gap_h = random.randint(_CELL_GAP_RANGE[0], max(_CELL_GAP_RANGE[0], max_h - 2))\n        gap_w = random.randint(_CELL_GAP_RANGE[0], max(_CELL_GAP_RANGE[0], max_w - 2))\n        h, w = gap_h + 2, gap_w + 2\n        walls = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=bool)\n        walls[0, :] = True\n        walls[h - 1, :] = True\n        walls[:, 0] = True\n        walls[:, w - 1] = True\n        return walls\n\n    # For target_rooms >= 2: grid of cells, then selectively merge\n    possible_configs = []\n    for nv in range(2, 6):\n        for nh in range(2, 5):\n            cells = (nv - 1) * (nh - 1)\n            if target_rooms <= cells <= target_rooms + 4:\n                possible_configs.append((nv, nh, cells))\n    if not possible_configs:\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(_GEN_RETRIES):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {_GEN_RETRIES} attempts\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
